      "eyebrow": "Worked review",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "Start with DNS resolution and the intended endpoint address. Inspect the used subnet route-table association, matching destination route and usable target, then protocol/port rules and response traffic. A connection symptom alone does not identify the failing layer.",
          "role": "example",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#tb4",
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#tb5"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#f1",
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#f2",
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#f3",
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#f4"
            ],
            "segment_ids": [],
            "anchors": []
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "If the supplied evidence identifies a missing response allowance in a network ACL, use the approved correction for that layer. A permitted request direction is not sufficient for a stateless ACL. Retest the endpoint connection after correction rather than altering every control or accepting an edited route table as proof of the used path.",
          "role": "example",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#tb4",
              "aws_devops.diagnose_aws_connectivity_failures#tb5"
            ],
